From fc15436c9e5cd5244a9e10dee4f5e860e4969beb Mon Sep 17 00:00:00 2001
From: zirize <zirize@gmail.com>
Date: Wed, 16 Sep 2026 05:24:11 +0900
Subject: [PATCH 4/9] ui/sdl: do not scale relative pointer motion

handle_mousemotion() converts the pointer position from window to guest
coordinates, which is right - it is a point on the screen and the guest
has to be told where on its own screen that is. It scales the relative
motion the same way, which is not: relative motion is what the mouse
reports, and a mouse knows nothing about the resolution the guest
happens to be in.

The factor is below one whenever the window is larger than the guest,
which is the usual case for the low resolution guests SDL tends to be
used for. At a 3x zoom the motion is divided by three, so the mouse has
to be moved three times as far as it would be on hardware, and each
event is truncated to a whole guest pixel on the way. It also means
enlarging the window makes the pointer slower, which is not something a
relative pointing device should do.

The scaling came in with commit 30aa105640 ("ui/sdl: Consider scaling in
mouse event handling"), whose subject and body are about positions;
before it the relative motion was passed through unchanged. Restore
that and leave it to the guest to turn motion into cursor movement.

Signed-off-by: zirize <zirize@gmail.com>
Co-Authored-By: Claude Opus 5 (1M context) <noreply@anthropic.com>
---
 ui/sdl2.c | 12 ++++++++++--
 1 file changed, 10 insertions(+), 2 deletions(-)

diff --git a/ui/sdl2.c b/ui/sdl2.c
index 1f5cd68ecb..4749d37061 100644
--- a/ui/sdl2.c
+++ b/ui/sdl2.c
@@ -520,8 +520,16 @@ static void handle_mousemotion(SDL_Event *ev)
     surf_h = surface_height(scon->surface);
     x = (int64_t)ev->motion.x * surf_w / scr_w;
     y = (int64_t)ev->motion.y * surf_h / scr_h;
-    dx = (int64_t)ev->motion.xrel * surf_w / scr_w;
-    dy = (int64_t)ev->motion.yrel * surf_h / scr_h;
+
+    /*
+     * The position above is a point on the screen and has to be expressed in
+     * the guest's pixels, but relative motion is not: it is what the mouse
+     * reports, and a mouse knows nothing about the resolution the guest is
+     * in. Pass it through and leave it to the guest to turn it into cursor
+     * movement, the way it would on hardware.
+     */
+    dx = ev->motion.xrel;
+    dy = ev->motion.yrel;
     if (gui_grab || qemu_input_is_absolute(scon->dcl.con) || absolute_enabled) {
         sdl_send_mouse_event(scon, dx, dy, x, y, ev->motion.state);
     }
